// Nouns describes each noun the methods' commands have.
func Nouns() []apicli.Noun {
	return []apicli.Noun{
		{Name: "api-tokens", Short: "Create, list or revoke API tokens", Long: "TokenService manages API tokens: secrets for scripts, the CLI and integrations,\neach acting for the person who made it, within a scope. See\ndocs/adr/0016-scoped-api-tokens.md.\n\nSend a token in the Authorization header, as \"Bearer dwt_…\", with REST, Connect,\ngRPC or gRPC-Web alike. A read-only token may call the methods whose effect is\nEFFECT_READ; a read-write token may also call EFFECT_PLAN and EFFECT_APPLY\nmethods. No token may call an EFFECT_DESTRUCTIVE method, AuthService, or this\nservice: they need a signed-in session."},
		{Name: "auth-state", Short: "Say whether the install has an owner", Long: "The install's auth state says whether it has an owner yet, who is signed in on a\nrequest, and which address passkeys are made for there. dockwell auth-state get\nneeds no sign-in."},
		{Name: "changes", Short: "Propose, get, list, apply, watch, undo or discard changes", Long: "A change is a set of edits to managed state, with the plan that carries them\nout. You propose edits and get a plan back; applying it writes immutable\nrevisions and runs its steps on the servers; undoing it makes a new plan that\nputs things back. A change applies only as it was planned, and a plan never\nshows a secret, only that it changed.\n\nSee docs/adr/0009-resources-revisions-and-plans.md and\ndocs/adr/0017-the-change-api-and-the-agent-stream.md."},
		{Name: "claim-link", Short: "Check, begin or finish claim link", Long: "A claim link, which dockwell run and dockwell claim print, creates the install's\nowner with a passkey, and works once. dockwell claim-link check describes the\nserver a link claims, and dockwell claim-link begin and dockwell claim-link\nfinish make the passkey, which takes a browser: open the link in one."},
		{Name: "compose", Short: "Import or export compose", Long: "ComposeService turns a Docker Compose file into an environment's services,\nvolumes and networks, and any of them back into one. An environment is one\nCompose project: its containers carry the labels Docker Compose gives its own,\nso docker compose sees them, and an exported file runs the same with docker\ncompose alone (PRD principle 6). See docs/adr/0021-the-docker-driver.md."},
		{Name: "domains", Short: "Return the domains of the routes that exist now", Long: "DomainService says where the domains of your routes stand: what each sends\nwhere, and whether its certificate is pending, issued or failed, with what to do\nwhen it failed. Change routes with ChangeService, as kind \"route\". See\ndocs/adr/0024-the-caddy-ingress-driver.md."},
		{Name: "drift", Short: "List, revert or adopt drift", Long: "Drift is a difference between what a server has and the latest revision of a\nresource it should have, as when someone changed it by hand. dockwell drift list\nasks each connected server's agent, and changes nothing. dockwell drift revert\nplans putting the revision back, and dockwell drift adopt plans keeping what the\nserver has as a new revision: each is a change, with a plan to apply."},
		{Name: "events", Short: "List or watch events", Long: "EventService is the timeline: every change to managed state, with who made it\nand from where, beside what servers and people did that belongs with it, such as\na server joining or someone signing in. See docs/adr/0020-the-event-timeline.md.\n\nA change is one event, which moves on as the change does: planned, then\napplying, then applied or failed. Rollbacks and undos are changes of their own,\neach naming the change it reverts. A discarded change isn't on the timeline, and\nnor is the change that set up an install's default project and environment.\nEvery other event comes from the audit log, as it was recorded.\n\nEvents carry no secret, no spec and no address.\n\ndockwell events watch streams, so it has no REST path. A REST client polls GET\n/v1/events, or opens the stream with Connect, gRPC or gRPC-Web, with the same\ncredentials."},
		{Name: "passkeys", Short: "List, begin add, finish add or remove passkeys", Long: "Passkeys are how you sign in to the panel. A browser, a phone or a security key\nmakes each one, for one address, and only it can use it. Adding and removing\nthem needs a signed-in session, which no API token gives, and a passkey you\nalready have to confirm it, so you do it in the panel, under Profile and\nsecurity."},
		{Name: "resources", Short: "Return the resources that exist now", Long: "ResourceService reads the resources Dockwell manages: projects, environments,\nservices, volumes, networks, routes, containers and firewall rules. Change them\nwith ChangeService.\n\nIt names resources and says where they live, and never carries a spec, which may\nhold secrets. A spec's display_name, which never does, is the one exception."},
		{Name: "secrets", Short: "Reveal, create, update or delete secrets", Long: "A secret is a value of an environment that its services use without anyone\nreading it back, such as a database password. Dockwell keeps it encrypted, sends\nit only to the servers that run a service that uses it, and never shows it in\nplans, history or logs. Its value is read from standard input or a file, never\nfrom a flag. Revealing it takes a passkey, in the panel, and is audited: no API\ntoken can.\n\ndockwell secrets create, update and delete each propose a change, show its plan,\nand ask before applying it."},
		{Name: "servers", Short: "List, get or watch servers", Long: "ServerService lists the servers Dockwell manages and streams their live state,\nas each server's agent reports it.\n\ndockwell servers watch has no REST path: REST has no way to stream messages, so\na REST client polls dockwell servers list or dockwell servers get instead, and\nanything that wants the stream opens it with Connect, gRPC or gRPC-Web. See\ndocs/adr/0015-rest-and-openapi-from-the-schema.md."},
		{Name: "sessions", Short: "List or revoke sessions, and 8 more", Long: "A session is a browser you're signed in on. Signing in takes a passkey, a code\nfrom an authenticator app, a recovery code, or a one-time link from dockwell\nrecover, and sets a session cookie. No API token can sign in, or list or end\nsessions: how you sign in changes only by signing in, in the panel."},
		{Name: "system", Short: "Return the build of the control plane that answers", Long: "SystemService describes the Dockwell install itself. It needs no sign-in, so the\nCLI, agents and other tools can check what they are talking to first."},
		{Name: "totp", Short: "Get, disable or unlock TOTP, and 4 more", Long: "Code sign-in lets you sign in with a code from an authenticator app, or with a\none-time recovery code, beside your passkeys. Wrong codes slow it down, and too\nmany lock it until dockwell totp unlock turns it back on. Setting it up needs a\nsigned-in session, which no API token gives, and your passkey to confirm it, so\nyou do it in the panel, under Profile and security."},
		{Name: "variables", Short: "List, create, update or delete variables", Long: "A variable is a value of an environment that its services use, such as\nLOG_LEVEL=info. It isn't secret: plans and the panel show it. Services use\nvariables and secrets by reference, under the names they expect, so changing one\nis a plan that restarts every service that uses it.\n\ndockwell variables create, update and delete each propose a change, show its\nplan, and ask before applying it."},
	}
}

// Kinds describes the commands for each kind of resource an edit's spec
// can hold.
func Kinds() []*apicli.Kind {
	return []*apicli.Kind{
		{
			Field: "project",
			Noun:  "projects",
			Short: "A project, kind \"project\"",
			Long:  "Project is a product or client in the organization. It holds environments, and\nscopes members, secrets and domains. An install starts with one, named\n\"default\", which stays out of sight until there is a second.",
			Fields: []apicli.Field{
				{Path: "display_name", Help: "How the panel shows it, such as \"Acme web\". Empty shows its name, which URLs and the CLI use."},
			},
		},
		{
			Field: "environment",
			Noun:  "environments",
			Short: "An environment, kind \"environment\"",
			Long:  "Environment is a stage of a project, such as production or staging, with its own\nconfig. A project starts with one, named \"production\", which stays out of sight\nuntil there is a second.",
			Fields: []apicli.Field{
				{Path: "display_name", Help: "How the panel shows it, such as \"Staging\". Empty shows its name."},
			},
		},
		{
			Field: "service",
			Noun:  "services",
			Short: "A service, kind \"service\"",
			Long:  "Service is anything that runs in an environment, placed on servers: for now an\napp from an image, run as one container on each server it is placed on, with the\nlabels Docker Compose gives a service's container, so docker compose sees it. A\nCompose file becomes services, volumes and networks. Websites, databases,\ncatalog apps and jobs come later. Where it runs isn't part of the spec: each\nrevision is deployed on its servers.\n\nA service uses its environment's variables and secrets by reference: the\nenvironment holds each value once, and a service names the ones it uses, under\nthe names it expects. So changing a value changes every service that uses it, in\none plan.",
			Fields: []apicli.Field{
				{Path: "app.image", Help: "The image to run, such as \"ghcr.io/acme/web:1.4.2\"."},
				{Path: "app.command", Help: "Overrides the image's command when set."},
				{Path: "app.env", Help: "Environment variables, which may hold secrets.", Sensitive: true},
				{Path: "app.ports", Help: "Ports published on the server."},
				{Path: "app.restart", Help: "What Docker does when the container stops."},
				{Path: "app.labels", Help: "Labels on the container, for tools that read them. Keys that start with \"com.docker.compose.\" or \"dev.dockwell.\" are Dockwell's own."},
				{Path: "app.entrypoint", Help: "Overrides the image's entrypoint when set. Empty takes the image's; no_entrypoint runs none."},
				{Path: "app.mounts", Help: "Volumes and server paths mounted into the container."},
				{Path: "app.networks", Help: "The networks the container joins, by name. A service's are networks in its environment; a container's on its own are Docker's. Empty joins Docker's default bridge network."},
				{Path: "app.user", Help: "The user the command runs as, such as \"1000\" or \"node\". Empty takes the image's."},
				{Path: "app.working_dir", Help: "The directory the command runs in. Empty takes the image's."},
				{Path: "app.healthcheck.test", Help: "The check, as Docker takes it: [\"CMD\", \"curl\", \"-f\", \"http://localhost\"], [\"CMD-SHELL\", \"curl -f http://localhost || exit 1\"], or [\"NONE\"] to turn off the image's check."},
				{Path: "app.healthcheck.interval", Help: "How often it runs, such as \"30s\". Empty takes Docker's default."},
				{Path: "app.healthcheck.timeout", Help: "How long one check may take, such as \"5s\". Empty takes Docker's default."},
				{Path: "app.healthcheck.start_period", Help: "How long the container has to start before failures count, such as \"1m\". Empty takes Docker's default."},
				{Path: "app.healthcheck.retries", Help: "How many failures in a row make it unhealthy. 0 takes Docker's default."},
				{Path: "app.no_entrypoint", Help: "Runs no entrypoint, not even the image's, as Compose's \"entrypoint: []\" does: the command runs on its own. Takes no entrypoint beside it."},
				{Path: "app.external_networks", Help: "Networks a service joins that Dockwell didn't make, by their names in Docker, such as \"1panel-network\". Each must be on the server already: Dockwell never makes, changes or deletes one. Whatever else is on it can reach the service, so joining one is host access, which a person approves. A container on its own names Docker's networks in networks."},
				{Path: "app.host_network", Help: "Runs a service on the server's own network, with none of its own, as Compose's \"network_mode: host\" does. It is host access, and takes no networks, ports or aliases. A container on its own names \"host\" in networks."},
				{Path: "app.aliases", Help: "Other names containers on a service's networks reach it by, beside its service's name, such as a Compose file's container_name. No two services on a network can answer to one name."},
				{Path: "app.resources.cpus", Help: "The most CPUs it may use, such as \"0.5\" or \"2\", to three decimal places. Empty has no limit."},
				{Path: "app.resources.memory", Help: "The most memory it may use, such as \"512M\" or \"2G\", from 6M, in units of 1024. Empty has no limit."},
				{Path: "app.resources.memory_reservation", Help: "The memory Docker tries to leave it when the server runs short, such as \"256M\", from 6M and no more than memory. Empty reserves none."},
				{Path: "app.cap_add", Help: "Linux capabilities it gets beyond Docker's default ones, such as \"NET_ADMIN\", or \"ALL\". It is host access."},
				{Path: "app.cap_drop", Help: "Linux capabilities it gives up, such as \"NET_RAW\", or \"ALL\"."},
				{Path: "app.group_add", Help: "Groups its command joins beside its user's, by name or number, such as \"audio\" or \"999\". It is host access: joining the group that owns Docker's socket is the same as root."},
				{Path: "app.privileged", Help: "Runs it with every device and capability on the server, the same as root. It is host access."},
				{Path: "app.restart_retries", Help: "How many times on-failure restarts it before giving up. 0 has no limit. Only on-failure takes one."},
				{Path: "variables", Help: "The environment's variables the service uses, each under the name the service sees it as: DATABASE_HOST=DB_HOST gives the service the variable DB_HOST as DATABASE_HOST. An empty value means the variable of the same name."},
				{Path: "secrets", Help: "The environment's secrets the service uses, named as for variables. Using a secret doesn't let anyone read it: its value goes only to the servers the service runs on, and only their agents decrypt it."},
			},
		},
		{
			Field: "container",
			Noun:  "containers",
			Short: "A container run by hand on a server, kind \"container\"",
			Long:  "Container is one Docker container: run by hand on a server, or a service's app.\nDocker fills in from the image what a container leaves out, such as its command\nor working directory, and that isn't drift.",
			Fields: []apicli.Field{
				{Path: "image", Help: "The image to run, such as \"ghcr.io/acme/web:1.4.2\"."},
				{Path: "command", Help: "Overrides the image's command when set."},
				{Path: "env", Help: "Environment variables, which may hold secrets.", Sensitive: true},
				{Path: "ports", Help: "Ports published on the server."},
				{Path: "restart", Help: "What Docker does when the container stops."},
				{Path: "labels", Help: "Labels on the container, for tools that read them. Keys that start with \"com.docker.compose.\" or \"dev.dockwell.\" are Dockwell's own."},
				{Path: "entrypoint", Help: "Overrides the image's entrypoint when set. Empty takes the image's; no_entrypoint runs none."},
				{Path: "mounts", Help: "Volumes and server paths mounted into the container."},
				{Path: "networks", Help: "The networks the container joins, by name. A service's are networks in its environment; a container's on its own are Docker's. Empty joins Docker's default bridge network."},
				{Path: "user", Help: "The user the command runs as, such as \"1000\" or \"node\". Empty takes the image's."},
				{Path: "working_dir", Help: "The directory the command runs in. Empty takes the image's."},
				{Path: "healthcheck.test", Help: "The check, as Docker takes it: [\"CMD\", \"curl\", \"-f\", \"http://localhost\"], [\"CMD-SHELL\", \"curl -f http://localhost || exit 1\"], or [\"NONE\"] to turn off the image's check."},
				{Path: "healthcheck.interval", Help: "How often it runs, such as \"30s\". Empty takes Docker's default."},
				{Path: "healthcheck.timeout", Help: "How long one check may take, such as \"5s\". Empty takes Docker's default."},
				{Path: "healthcheck.start_period", Help: "How long the container has to start before failures count, such as \"1m\". Empty takes Docker's default."},
				{Path: "healthcheck.retries", Help: "How many failures in a row make it unhealthy. 0 takes Docker's default."},
				{Path: "no_entrypoint", Help: "Runs no entrypoint, not even the image's, as Compose's \"entrypoint: []\" does: the command runs on its own. Takes no entrypoint beside it."},
				{Path: "external_networks", Help: "Networks a service joins that Dockwell didn't make, by their names in Docker, such as \"1panel-network\". Each must be on the server already: Dockwell never makes, changes or deletes one. Whatever else is on it can reach the service, so joining one is host access, which a person approves. A container on its own names Docker's networks in networks."},
				{Path: "host_network", Help: "Runs a service on the server's own network, with none of its own, as Compose's \"network_mode: host\" does. It is host access, and takes no networks, ports or aliases. A container on its own names \"host\" in networks."},
				{Path: "aliases", Help: "Other names containers on a service's networks reach it by, beside its service's name, such as a Compose file's container_name. No two services on a network can answer to one name."},
				{Path: "resources.cpus", Help: "The most CPUs it may use, such as \"0.5\" or \"2\", to three decimal places. Empty has no limit."},
				{Path: "resources.memory", Help: "The most memory it may use, such as \"512M\" or \"2G\", from 6M, in units of 1024. Empty has no limit."},
				{Path: "resources.memory_reservation", Help: "The memory Docker tries to leave it when the server runs short, such as \"256M\", from 6M and no more than memory. Empty reserves none."},
				{Path: "cap_add", Help: "Linux capabilities it gets beyond Docker's default ones, such as \"NET_ADMIN\", or \"ALL\". It is host access."},
				{Path: "cap_drop", Help: "Linux capabilities it gives up, such as \"NET_RAW\", or \"ALL\"."},
				{Path: "group_add", Help: "Groups its command joins beside its user's, by name or number, such as \"audio\" or \"999\". It is host access: joining the group that owns Docker's socket is the same as root."},
				{Path: "privileged", Help: "Runs it with every device and capability on the server, the same as root. It is host access."},
				{Path: "restart_retries", Help: "How many times on-failure restarts it before giving up. 0 has no limit. Only on-failure takes one."},
			},
		},
		{
			Field: "firewall_rule",
			Noun:  "firewall-rules",
			Short: "A firewall rule on a server, kind \"firewall.rule\"",
			Long:  "FirewallRule allows or blocks inbound traffic to a port on a server.",
			Fields: []apicli.Field{
				{Path: "action", Help: "Allow or block."},
				{Path: "protocol", Help: "TCP unless set."},
				{Path: "port", Help: "The port the rule covers."},
				{Path: "sources", Help: "Source addresses in CIDR form, such as \"203.0.113.0/24\". Empty means anywhere."},
				{Path: "comment", Help: "Why the rule exists, shown next to it."},
			},
		},
		{
			Field: "volume",
			Noun:  "volumes",
			Short: "A volume in an environment, kind \"volume\"",
			Long:  "Volume is a Docker volume in an environment, which its services mount by name,\nas a Compose file's volumes. Deleting one destroys its data, though Dockwell\nkeeps it for 7 days first. Docker can't change a volume, so its spec can't\nchange once it is made.",
			Fields: []apicli.Field{
				{Path: "driver", Help: "The volume driver: \"local\" unless set."},
				{Path: "options", Help: "The driver's options, such as type, device and o for an NFS mount. They may hold secrets, as a CIFS mount's password goes in o. The local driver takes type none with bind, nfs, nfs4, cifs or tmpfs.", Sensitive: true},
				{Path: "labels", Help: "Labels on the volume, for tools that read them."},
			},
		},
		{
			Field: "network",
			Noun:  "networks",
			Short: "A network in an environment, kind \"network\"",
			Long:  "Network is a Docker network in an environment, which its services join by name,\nas a Compose file's networks. A service reaches another on a network they share\nby the other's name. Docker can't change a network, so its spec can't change\nonce it is made.",
			Fields: []apicli.Field{
				{Path: "driver", Help: "The network driver: \"bridge\" unless set."},
				{Path: "options", Help: "The driver's options."},
				{Path: "internal", Help: "Cuts the network off from outside the server: only its containers reach each other."},
				{Path: "labels", Help: "Labels on the network, for tools that read them."},
			},
		},
		{
			Field: "variable",
			Noun:  "variables",
			Short: "A variable of an environment, kind \"variable\"",
			Long:  "Variable is a value of an environment that its services use, such as\nLOG_LEVEL=info. It isn't secret: plans, history and the panel show it. Keep\nanything that is in a Secret.",
			Fields: []apicli.Field{
				{Path: "value", Help: "The value."},
			},
		},
		{
			Field: "secret",
			Noun:  "secrets",
			Short: "A secret of an environment, kind \"secret\"",
			Long:  "Secret is a value of an environment that its services use without anyone reading\nit back, such as a database password. Dockwell keeps it encrypted, sends it only\nto the servers that run a service that uses it, and never shows it in plans,\nhistory or logs. Revealing it takes a passkey, and is audited.",
			Fields: []apicli.Field{
				{Path: "value", Help: "The value. Every edit sets it whole.", Sensitive: true},
			},
		},
		{
			Field: "route",
			Noun:  "routes",
			Short: "A domain or path sent to a service, kind \"route\"",
			Long:  "Route sends a domain's traffic, or a path under it, to a port of a service in\nthe route's environment, or to a port on the server itself. It comes over HTTPS\nwith a certificate Dockwell gets for the domain, unless tls says TLS ends before\nthis server. A server's first route starts its ingress, which takes ports 80 and\n443, or port 80 alone while no route on the server takes a certificate; its last\nstops it and frees them. See docs/adr/0024-the-caddy-ingress-driver.md and\ndocs/adr/0030-routes-behind-a-tunnel-or-a-proxy.md.",
			Fields: []apicli.Field{
				{Path: "domain", Help: "The domain, such as \"app.example.com\": a lowercase DNS name that a public certificate authority issues for. Wildcards aren't taken."},
				{Path: "path", Help: "The path it covers, such as \"/api\": that path and what is under it, which reach the service as they are. Empty covers the whole domain."},
				{Path: "service", Help: "The service to send it to, by name, in the route's environment. Empty when host_port names a port on the server instead."},
				{Path: "port", Help: "The port the service listens on inside its container, such as 8080. Empty when host_port names a port on the server instead."},
				{Path: "tls", Help: "\"off\" when TLS ends before this server, as at a tunnel or a proxy in front of it: the domain is served over plain HTTP on port 80, with no certificate and no redirect to HTTPS, so its traffic reaches this server unencrypted. Such a route answers only the proxies in trusted_proxies, or with none, this server's own loopback, unless plain_for_anyone says otherwise. Anyone else gets what a name with no route gets. Empty gets a certificate. A domain's routes all take the same setting. A person approves taking a domain that has had a certificate off it."},
				{Path: "trusted_proxies", Help: "The proxies in front of this server whose word this route takes on where a request came from, each an address, a range such as \"203.0.113.0/24\", or \"cloudflare\" for Cloudflare's published ranges. A request from anywhere else has its address taken from the connection, whatever another route on the server names. From an address or a range, X-Forwarded-For is read from the right. From \"cloudflare\", CF-Connecting-IP is read, as Cloudflare documents, and it's only as sure as Cloudflare says: see docs/security/ingress.md. A person approves each one a change adds, as a proxy that isn't really one makes client addresses forgeable. A range of Cloudflare's is named by the preset, and a public range wider than /12, or /28 in IPv6, or a private one wider than /8, isn't taken."},
				{Path: "host_port", Help: "A port on the server itself, such as 8080, in place of service and port, for something that isn't a Dockwell service, or a service on the server's own network, which a route can't send to by name. The proxy dials 127.0.0.1 on it, so what's there sees every caller as local. It reaches past containers, so a person approves it, and any change to where it answers. Dockwell's own ports, and Docker's API on 2375 and 2376, aren't taken."},
				{Path: "plain_for_anyone", Help: "True for a route that says tls off to answer anyone who can reach this server's port 80, as for a site on a private network, or a load balancer whose addresses aren't known. It goes around whatever is in front, so a person approves it."},
			},
		},
	}
}
